For any neighbourhood system and any order isomorphism from the closed unit interval of reals onto its elements, the image of a point t with 0 < t ≤ 1 is not compact.

Suppose it is compact. The points of the unit interval whose value lies in [0, t) form a nonempty set, since 0 belongs to it. Their images are a directed family, because the maximum of two such values still lies in [0, t) and the isomorphism is monotone. That set of points has least upper bound t, by the least-upper-bound property of [0, t), so the image of the set has least upper bound the image of t. The range of the family equals that image, and the directed supremum is the least upper bound of the range, so the directed supremum equals the image of t. Compactness supplies a member of the family above the image of t. The inverse order of the isomorphism then puts t at most that member's value, contradicting that the member's value is strictly less than t.
